13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 2–19, 1864","place":"Southwestern Virginia and New Market in the Shenandoah Valley","result":"Breckinridge defeated Sigel at New Market and sent about 2,500 troops to reinforce Lee. Crook and Averell had already withdrawn to West Virginia, so Grant’s western Virginia operations no longer kept Confederate forces away from the Army of Northern Virginia","facts":[{"label":"U.S. troops engaged at New Market","value":"6,275"},{"label":"Confederate troops engaged","value":"4,090"},{"label":"VMI cadets in the episode’s count","value":"227"},{"label":"U.S. casualties","value":"841","note":"96 killed, 520 wounded, and 225 missing"},{"label":"Confederate casualties","value":"At least 520"},{"label":"VMI cadet casualties","value":"55","note":"10 killed and 45 wounded in the episode’s count"},{"label":"U.S. guns captured","value":"5"},{"label":"Troops sent to Lee afterward","value":"About 2,500"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop positions and movements at New Market","caption":"The battlefield survey marks the Valley Turnpike, Bushong’s Hill, Shirley’s Hill, the opposing lines, artillery, the VMI cadet movement, and the time of successive advances on May 15.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448860/","image":"/images/new-market-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed historical map of the Battle of New Market showing troop positions and times"},{"title":"New Market in the Shenandoah Valley today","caption":"The battlefield lies between the North Fork of the Shenandoah River and Smith Creek, along the Valley Turnpike south of Mount Jackson. These waterways limited both armies’ room to maneuver.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-78.83%2C38.55%2C-78.52%2C38.83&layer=mapnik&marker=38.6576%2C-78.6714"},{"title":"The wider 1864 Virginia campaign","caption":"Sigel’s force advanced south through the Shenandoah Valley while Crook and Averell operated against the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad farther southwest. Their withdrawals allowed Breckinridge to move east by rail toward Lee.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the 1864 campaign in Virginia"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:39","audioTime":99,"title":"Crook returned to West Virginia after Cloyd’s Mountain","detail":"His column reached Union on May 14. Averell’s cavalry arrived the next day after abandoning its own objectives in southwestern Virginia."},{"time":"2:07","audioTime":127,"title":"Averell began his raid from Charleston","detail":"The cavalry crossed difficult mountain country and reached Jeffersonville on May 8. Reports exaggerated the Confederate force protecting the Saltville salt works."},{"time":"2:52","audioTime":172,"title":"Averell turned from Saltville and was stopped near Wytheville","detail":"William E. Jones and John Hunt Morgan attacked with about 4,500 Confederates and repeatedly forced the U.S. cavalry back during a four-hour fight."},{"time":"3:52","audioTime":232,"title":"Averell reached the railroad after Crook","detail":"At Dublin Depot he found damage already caused by Crook. He destroyed several miles of track near Christiansburg but did not continue east toward Lynchburg as Crook directed."},{"time":"5:12","audioTime":312,"title":"Crook reported substantial tactical results","detail":"His force destroyed the Dublin bridge, eighteen miles of railroad, depots, and stores, captured ten guns and about three hundred prisoners, and defeated the Confederate force at Cloyd’s Mountain."},{"time":"6:42","audioTime":402,"title":"The raids no longer supported Grant’s main campaign","detail":"By May 11 both western columns were withdrawing. They were no longer threatening Confederate infrastructure or preventing troops from moving to Lee."},{"time":"7:22","audioTime":442,"title":"False reports influenced Crook’s withdrawal","detail":"Captured dispatches incorrectly said Grant had been defeated and was retreating. Crook expected reinforcements from Lee to arrive by railroad and chose not to continue."},{"time":"8:40","audioTime":520,"title":"Sigel advanced up the Shenandoah Valley","detail":"His force of nearly 9,000 used the Valley Turnpike. The campaign threatened the region’s food production and the railroad facilities at Staunton."},{"time":"10:29","audioTime":629,"title":"Sigel left Cedar Creek on May 11","detail":"Captured messages at Woodstock told him that Breckinridge was approaching with roughly 4,000 to 4,500 troops. John D. Imboden’s cavalry and local home guards delayed the advance."},{"time":"11:14","audioTime":674,"title":"A reconnaissance advanced twenty miles to New Market","detail":"August Moor led 2,350 troops unfamiliar to him and reached the town on May 14 after pushing back Imboden’s cavalry."},{"time":"12:25","audioTime":745,"title":"Breckinridge’s infantry arrived south of New Market","detail":"U.S. patrols reported the concentration around 7 a.m. on May 15. Julius Stahel took command of the 3,350 U.S. troops then at New Market while Sigel approached."},{"time":"13:11","audioTime":791,"title":"Sigel marched from Woodstock","detail":"He left at 5 a.m. on May 15 with the rest of the column but had not concentrated every unit before the main battle began."},{"time":"14:35","audioTime":875,"title":"Breckinridge assembled a mixed force","detail":"His command included Echols’s and Wharton’s infantry, Imboden’s cavalry, home guards, engineers, eighteen guns, and 227 cadets from the Virginia Military Institute."},{"time":"15:42","audioTime":942,"title":"Breckinridge decided to attack","detail":"The North Fork of the Shenandoah and Smith Creek protected his flanks. He concluded that his smaller force could advance without being easily enveloped."},{"time":"16:02","audioTime":962,"title":"Confederate countermarching exaggerated troop strength","detail":"Units moved repeatedly on Shirley’s Hill to appear more numerous. U.S. estimates rose as high as 20,000, increasing uncertainty in Sigel’s headquarters."},{"time":"17:13","audioTime":1033,"title":"Sigel formed two disconnected lines","detail":"He placed one line on Bushong’s Hill and another about one thousand yards forward. Some units were still following earlier orders to concentrate at Mount Jackson."},{"time":"18:12","audioTime":1092,"title":"Confederate artillery struck the U.S. cavalry flank","detail":"Imboden placed guns east of Smith Creek while Breckinridge advanced his infantry and moved the main artillery forward from hill to hill."},{"time":"19:22","audioTime":1162,"title":"The first U.S. line withdrew","detail":"Echols and Wharton overlapped it on both flanks and followed closely toward Bushong’s Hill. Terrain, smoke, and the rapid advance opened a gap in the Confederate center."},{"time":"20:38","audioTime":1238,"title":"Breckinridge committed the VMI cadets","detail":"The cadet battalion was his last available reserve. He ordered it into the gap opposite Stahel’s cavalry to prevent the Confederate line from being divided."},{"time":"22:26","audioTime":1346,"title":"The cadets entered the main battle line","detail":"Musket and artillery fire caused immediate casualties, but the battalion maintained formation and closed the gap near the Bushong orchard."},{"time":"24:29","audioTime":1469,"title":"Sigel ordered an understrength counterattack","detail":"About 1,700 U.S. soldiers attacked between 2,600 and 2,800 Confederates. The failed charge disrupted Sigel’s line and gave Breckinridge the opportunity to advance again."},{"time":"25:28","audioTime":1528,"title":"The Confederate advance captured five guns","detail":"VMI cadets took one twelve-pounder Napoleon and other Confederate units captured four additional pieces as U.S. artillery tried to withdraw."},{"time":"26:07","audioTime":1567,"title":"Sigel ordered a general retreat","detail":"Imboden did not seize the single bridge over the swollen river, allowing the U.S. force to cross beginning around 4:30 p.m."},{"time":"26:27","audioTime":1587,"title":"The U.S. column retreated thirty-two miles","detail":"The withdrawal continued through Mount Jackson to Strasburg and ended the following day."},{"time":"27:20","audioTime":1640,"title":"New Market cost more than 1,300 casualties","detail":"The episode counts 841 U.S. casualties and at least 520 Confederate casualties. Nearly one third of Sigel’s full force never entered the battle."},{"time":"28:04","audioTime":1684,"title":"The VMI battalion suffered fifty-five casualties","detail":"Ten cadets were killed and forty-five wounded in the episode’s count, about one quarter of the battalion that entered the fight."},{"time":"29:15","audioTime":1755,"title":"Breckinridge sent troops to Lee","detail":"With Crook, Averell, and Sigel withdrawing, Breckinridge placed about 2,500 men from Echols’s and Wharton’s brigades on trains at Staunton on May 19."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Franz Sigel","command":"Department of West Virginia and Shenandoah Valley column","subordinates":"Julius Stahel’s cavalry and August Moor’s advance force"},{"commander":"Julius Stahel","command":"Cavalry division and senior officer at New Market","subordinates":"Took over Moor’s position before Sigel arrived"},{"commander":"August Moor","command":"Advance force","subordinates":"Three infantry regiments, about 1,000 cavalry, and an artillery battery"},{"commander":"George Crook","command":"Kanawha infantry column","subordinates":"Withdrew to West Virginia after the victory at Cloyd’s Mountain"},{"commander":"William W. Averell","command":"Cavalry raid","subordinates":"Turned away from Saltville and Wytheville and abandoned the advance toward Lynchburg"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"John C. Breckinridge","command":"Department of Western Virginia field force","subordinates":"Echols’s and Wharton’s brigades, Imboden’s cavalry, local troops, artillery, and the VMI cadets"},{"commander":"John Echols","command":"Infantry brigade","subordinates":"Advanced on the Confederate left toward Bushong’s Hill"},{"commander":"Gabriel C. Wharton","command":"Infantry brigade","subordinates":"Advanced with Echols in the main Confederate line"},{"commander":"John D. Imboden","command":"Cavalry brigade","subordinates":"Screened the approach, covered the Confederate right, and moved artillery east of Smith Creek"},{"commander":"Scott Shipp","command":"VMI Cadet Battalion","subordinates":"Led the cadets into the gap in Breckinridge’s center and was wounded"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"Failed to concentrate his larger force and ordered the retreat after his counterattack collapsed","side":"United States","image":"/images/franz-sigel.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666489/"},{"name":"August Moor","role":"Led the reconnaissance that occupied New Market before the main armies arrived","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Crook","role":"Won at Cloyd’s Mountain but withdrew after accepting a false report that Grant had been defeated","side":"United States"},{"name":"William W. Averell","role":"Abandoned the planned attacks on Saltville and the continued railroad movement toward Lynchburg","side":"United States"},{"name":"John C. Breckinridge","role":"Concentrated the Confederate force, chose to attack, and committed the VMI cadets to close a gap","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/john-c-breckinridge.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670277/"},{"name":"John D. Imboden","role":"Delayed Sigel and used cavalry and artillery to threaten the U.S. left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Scott Shipp","role":"Commanded the VMI cadets when they entered the Confederate battle line","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Nicholas James Bayard","role":"VMI cadet who fought at New Market; his wartime portrait represents the student battalion","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/vmi-cadet-nicholas-bayard.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630671/"}],"losses":[{"name":"Ten VMI cadets","side":"Confederacy","role":"Cadet Battalion, Virginia Military Institute","outcome":"Killed or mortally wounded during the advance across the New Market battlefield; another 45 cadets were wounded in the episode’s count"},{"name":"Thomas Garland Jefferson","side":"Confederacy","role":"Private, Company B, VMI Cadet Battalion","outcome":"Mortally wounded on May 15 and died at New Market on May 18 at age seventeen"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/new-market-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed survey map of New Market battlefield","caption":"The map records the successive lines and times of movement from Shirley’s Hill through New Market to Bushong’s Hill.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448860/"},{"image":"/images/new-market-battlefield-photo.jpg","alt":"Late nineteenth-century photograph of the New Market battlefield","caption":"The view shows the open ground crossed by the Confederate line and the VMI cadets, with Massanutten Mountain in the distance.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2019631455/"},{"image":"/images/franz-sigel.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Major General Franz Sigel","caption":"Sigel had the larger overall force but separated his lines and failed to bring nearly one third of his troops into action.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666489/"},{"image":"/images/vmi-cadet-nicholas-bayard.jpg","alt":"Portrait of VMI cadet Nicholas James Bayard","caption":"Bayard fought with the VMI Cadet Battalion at New Market. The battalion served as Breckinridge’s final reserve.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630671/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 488","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/03/31/civil-war-podcast-episode-488/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of New Market","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/battle-of-new-market.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: New Market battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va110"},{"label":"National Park Service: Thomas Garland Jefferson","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/thomas-garland-jefferson.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: New Market battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448860/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: New Market battlefield photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2019631455/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Nicholas James Bayard, VMI cadet","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630671/"}]}}]}]
